<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8" ?>
<!--
  Vespa refuses a schema change that alters how existing documents were
  indexed, because the documents already in the corpus keep the tokens they
  were written with. This file is how you tell it you understand that.

  WHAT IS OVERRIDDEN, AND WHY IT IS SAFE TO:

  `stemming: best -> none` on the indexed text fields (about, nip05, lud16,
  website, search_secondary, search_text, search_location). The indexing
  script goes from `tokenize normalize stem:"BEST"` to `tokenize normalize`.
  Nothing about the stored values changes — SearchExtractors and EventDoc are
  untouched — only how Vespa tokenizes them. So the fix is a Vespa REINDEX,
  which rebuilds the index from the documents already stored, and NOT a
  re-feed or a re-derivation of search fields.

  UNTIL THE REINDEX FINISHES, RECALL IS SPLIT: documents written before the
  deploy carry stemmed tokens and everything after carries unstemmed ones, so
  a query can match one and miss the other. On a 42.8M-document corpus that
  window is hours, not seconds. Deploy, then immediately:

      POST /application/v2/tenant/default/application/default/environment/prod\
           /region/default/instance/default/reindex?clusterId=content&documentType=event

  THIS FILE IS TRANSITIONAL. It only matters to a deployment UPGRADING across
  the stemming change — a fresh Vespa has no previous schema to compare
  against, so the validation never fires and the override is inert. Once every
  deployment has upgraded and reindexed, delete it; an override left lying
  around silently permits the NEXT indexing change too, which is the failure
  this mechanism exists to prevent.

  Vespa caps `until` at 30 days out and refuses to deploy past it, which is
  the forcing function for that cleanup.
-->
<validation-overrides>
    <allow until="2026-09-04" comment="stemming best->none on the indexed text fields; requires a reindex, see above">indexing-change</allow>
</validation-overrides>
